<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samoan Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Samoa Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Samoan Islands are an archipelago of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> in the central South Pacific, settled about 3,000 years ago. Rival chiefly titles competed for power there, with Tongan influence in some periods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripartite-convention-1899">Tripartite Convention of 1899</a> divided the islands: the western part, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a> and Savaii, became German Samoa, later administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and independent as Samoa in 1962, while the eastern part, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a> and Manuʻa, became American Samoa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samoan-islands.jpg" alt="Samoan Islands" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Angela K. Kepler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olosega_Aerial_AKK.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The archipelago covers 3,030 km2 in the central South Pacific and has 18 islands spread from west to east. It lies between 13° and 14° south latitude, about 800 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, 530 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>, 2,900 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and 4,000 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. The larger islands are volcanic, mountainous and covered in tropical moist forest, while some of the smaller ones are coral atolls with black sand beaches.</p><p>In the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a> (1,718 km2) is the most populous island and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savaii">Savaiʻi</a> (1,125 km2) the largest and highest, with Mt Silisili at 1,858 m. The small islands of Manono, Apolima and Nuʻulopa lie in the Apolima Strait between them, and the four Aleipata Islands lie off the east of Upolu. In the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a> is the largest island of American Samoa, and the Manuʻa group comprises Ofu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olosega">Olosega</a> and Taʻū. The islands may have been formed by the Samoa hotspot, though some evidence long seemed inconsistent with that theory until submarine samples gathered in 2005 gave older ages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest known evidence of human activity dates to around 1050 BCE, from a Lapita site at Mulifanua wharf on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a>. The inhabitants share the Samoan language, a culture called faʻa Samoa and an indigenous form of governance called faʻamatai. In 1768 the French explorer Bougainville visited the eastern islands and named them the Navigator Islands, a name that missionaries used until about 1845 and official European dispatches until about 1870.</p><p>In the late 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the United Kingdom competed for control of the islands. In 1892 the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malietoa-laupepa">Malietoa Laupepa</a> proclaimed that Monday 4 July would occur twice, moving the islands to the eastern side of the International Date Line, probably because of increasing trade with Americans. The Tripartite Convention of December 1899 partitioned the archipelago into German Samoa in the west and American Samoa in the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> began occupying the western islands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> and governed them under a League of Nations Class C Mandate from 1920 to 1946 and as a United Nations Trust Territory from 1946 to 1962. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mau-movement">Mau movement</a>, popular across the area, led to independence in 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The archipelago comprises all of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoa">Independent State of Samoa</a>, whose capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a>, and most of American Samoa, an unincorporated territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> whose capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pago-pago">Pago Pago</a>. The two jurisdictions are separated by 64 km of ocean at their closest points. Together they have approximately 250,000 inhabitants, and Samoans are one of the largest Polynesian populations in the world. Independent Samoa was known as Western Samoa until 1997. It moved back to the western side of the date line by skipping Friday 30 December 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samoan Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Māori moved from rural communities to the cities on a large scale after the Second World War, and from the 1960s migrants from <b>Samoa</b>, the Cook Islands, Tonga and Niue made Auckland the largest Polynesian city in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory remains unincorporated and unorganised; its people are US nationals rather than citizens, an arrangement many defend as protecting communal land and the faʻa <b>Samoa</b> way of life.</blockquote><a href="/state/american-samoa">American Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>Germany had dominated <b>Samoa</b>'s plantation economy since the 1850s through the firm Godeffroy and its successor, the DHPG.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-samoa">German Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>Manuʻa stood somewhat apart from the wars over titles in western <b>Samoa</b>, and its prestige rested on ancestry and ritual rather than conquest.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-manu-a">Kingdom of Manuʻa</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Samoa</b> was divided among districts and great titles such as Malietoa, Tupua Tamasese and Mataʻafa.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-samoa">Kingdom of Samoa</a></li><li><blockquote>Western <b>Samoa</b> became independent on 1 January 1962, the first Pacific island state to do so, and was renamed Samoa in 1997.</blockquote><a href="/state/territory-of-western-samoa">Territory of Western Samoa</a></li></ul></section>
